// pfodWeb NOTE: GPIO0 tagged "button" - confirmed onboard BOOT
// pushbutton (schematic component "Key2"). Official vendor schematic
// (files.waveshare.com/wiki/ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-2.8/
// ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-2.8.pdf) netlist shows Key2's signal pin tied
// directly to U5 (ESP32-S3R8) pin 5 = GPIO0, alongside pull-up R20 and
// debounce cap C28 - the standard BOOT-strap button topology. A
// separate "Key3" ties to the RESET/CHIP_PU net (not a GPIO, not
// taggable) and is the RESET button; left untagged.
// pfodWeb NOTE: GPIO6 tagged "button" - confirmed onboard "Battery
// Power Control Button" (schematic component "Key1", net name
// "Key_BAT", vendor Onboard-Resources item 13: "Battery Power Control
// Button - Relevant driver program is required"). Same schematic shows
// GPIO7 as the associated "BAT_Control" power-hold/enable output line,
// not a button or LED itself, and is left untagged (same input/control
// pin pattern independently confirmed on the sibling
// waveshare_esp32_s3_lcd_146 board's PWR_Key circuit).
// pfodWeb NOTE: No onboard LED/NeoPixel/RGB LED could be confirmed.
// The schematic shows exactly two onboard LEDs: LED1 ("Charging
// indicator") wired directly to the ETA6098 battery-charger IC's STAT
// pin (U1 pin 9) - not a general-purpose MCU GPIO; and LED2 ("Power
// indicator") wired directly across the 3V3/USB_5V rail - always on
// when powered, not GPIO-controlled. No WS2812/NeoPixel component
// appears anywhere in the schematic. See boardsDetails notes.txt for
// details, including two third-party community repos found via
// WebSearch that made unconfirmed/contradictory claims of a "RGB LED"
// on GPIO8 - GPIO8 is actually BAT_ADC per the vendor schematic and
// pin table, so that claim was rejected as unreliable.
